-- title:
--   One-step factorisation of the infinite-horizon canonical bandit model
-- statement:
--   The infinite-horizon canonical bandit model advances one round at a time through the one-round step kernel.
--
--   Let $\mathbb P_{\nu\pi}$ be the law of the infinite interaction trajectory of policy $\pi$ in environment $\nu$, and write $H_n=(A_1,X_1,\dots,A_n,X_n)$ for the first $n$ completed rounds. Then the joint law of the pair $\big(H_n,\ (A_{n+1},X_{n+1})\big)$ factorises as
--
--   $$
--   \mathrm{Law}\big(H_n,(A_{n+1},X_{n+1})\big) \;=\; \mathrm{Law}(H_n)\otimes K_n,
--   $$
--
--   where $K_n$ is the one-round step kernel: given the history, draw the arm from the policy and then the reward from that arm's distribution.
--
--   In words: conditionally on the first $n$ rounds, round $n+1$ is generated by the step kernel, exactly as in the finite-horizon canonical model. This is the structural bridge between the two canonical models of Section 4.6 — the finite-horizon one, defined by an explicit recursion, and the infinite-horizon one, obtained from the Ionescu-Tulcea theorem — and it is what allows a computation on the infinite trajectory space (a divergence, a martingale increment) to be carried out one round at a time.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Trajectory coordinate $t$ is round $t+1$, so `banditTrajPrefix k n` retains coordinates $0,\dots,n-1$ and the new observation is coordinate $n$. Mathlib states the corresponding fact for `Kernel.trajMeasure` with histories indexed by an initial segment `Iic a` of the naturals; the reconciliation with the `Fin n` indexing of the canonical model is part of the content.
-- source:
--   Lattimore and Szepesvari, Bandit Algorithms (CUP 2020), https://tor-lattimore.com/downloads/book/book.pdf : the canonical bandit model, Section 4.6, printed pp. 48-50, and its infinite-horizon form used in Section 33.2, printed pp. 405-406.

import Definitions.Def_BanditTrajectory

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

theorem BanditAlgorithm.banditTrajMeasure_joint_eq_compProd {k : ℕ}
    (ν : StochasticBandit k) (π : BanditPolicy k) (n : ℕ) :
    (banditTrajMeasure ν π).map (fun ω ↦ (banditTrajPrefix k n ω, ω n))
      = ((banditTrajMeasure ν π).map (banditTrajPrefix k n)) ⊗ₘ
        (banditStepKernel ν π n) := by
  sorry
